+/* MacGameFix: a DualSense or DualSense Edge on Bluetooth, shown to games as the
+ * same pad on a USB cable.
+ *
+ * Games that drive the pad themselves (Sony's PC library) speak the wired
+ * protocol, and give a Bluetooth pad less, or nothing: on a cable they find
+ * the haptics output by its container, which patch 0001 builds from the USB
+ * location. So the Bluetooth pad gets the wired pad's report descriptor and
+ * bus type, keeps its own location ID (macOS gives a Bluetooth pad one, the
+ * low four bytes of its address), and its reports are translated: input 0x31
+ * becomes USB 0x01, and the game's USB output 0x02 becomes Bluetooth 0x31 with
+ * its sequence and CRC.
+ *
+ * One thread per pad is the only writer to it, as a Bluetooth link drains
+ * only about 65 reports a second: the game's output, merged, at most every
+ * 16 ms; and the haptics. Those arrive on a socket from winecoreaudio.so, which
+ * shows the wired pad's 4-channel output for this pad and sends its channels 3
+ * and 4 at the pad's 3 kHz (see dualsense_bt.h there). While they play, the
+ * writer sends them 64 frames at a time in reports 0x32 (0x33 first), every
+ * 21.3 ms, and the game's output takes what is left of the link.
+ *
+ * The approach is NotProton PR #90's controller bridge, moved from interposed
+ * calls into this file. On only while ~/Library/Application Support/MacGameFix/
+ * pad.conf has the line "bluetooth=wired". */
